#only("1")[
#voiceover("The Efficient Market Hypothesis, or EMH, is a fundamental concept in traditional finance.")
]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("It states that asset prices fully reflect all available information at any given time.")
]
#only("3")[
#voiceover("This implies that it is impossible for investors to consistently achieve returns higher than the market average.")
]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Implications of EMH]
// The title "Implications of EMH" is shown on this slide
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[- Market is always "fairly" priced 🤝]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[- No consistent advantage for any investor 🙅]
#v(20pt)
#only("3-")[- Passive investing strategies recommended 📊]
// The implications of EMH are shown as bullet points from slide 1 onward
#only("1")[
#voiceover("If the market is efficient, it means that assets are always fairly priced based on the available information.")
]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("No investor can consistently gain an advantage over others by using publicly available information.")
]
#only("3")[
#voiceover("Therefore, EMH suggests that passive investing strategies, such as index funds, are the best approach for most investors.")
]
]

#slide()[
